# DECK 02 — the same shape as deck 01: slides are subchapters, `notes:` is the spoken script and
# never renders (chapter-model.ts skips keyed entries other than `description`). Every feature
# demoed here SHIPS today; the native mindmap and outliner editors are still planned
# (../../teasers/RU.md), so this deck shows a PlantUML chunk and the board instead.
# Specs: ../../CHAPTER.md, ../../TICKETS.md §3 (the board), ../../QUERY.md (finding things).
!!<*yamlover: $defs: chapter>
Mind-management in small doses
description: Capture, outline, decide, track, tag, find — six small jobs, six plain files
- Six small jobs a day throws at you
  notes: |
    Start on an empty folder in the app — nothing in it yet.

    Say: this is not a talk about a productivity system. It is about the six small jobs
    that a working day actually hands you. Something arrives and you have to CAPTURE it.
    A vague goal has to be OUTLINED into parts. Two options need a DECISION. Work in
    progress needs TRACKING. Things need to be FILED so they can be FOUND again.

    Most tools give you one of these and make you leave for the others. Here all six
    land in the same place — a folder of ordinary files — and I will do each one in
    about forty seconds.

    Point at the mindmap: and this diagram is not a picture I pasted. It is three lines
    of text inside the note, which is the third job. We will get there.
  - !!<format: text/x-plantuml> |
    @startmindmap
    * A working day
    ** Capture
    ** Outline
    ** Decide
    ** Track
    ** Tag
    ** Find
    @endmindmap
- Capture, then outline
  description: Type a flat list; press Tab; the indented line becomes a real folder on disk
  notes: |
    Create a note in the empty folder and type four or five short lines — whatever is on
    your mind, one thought per line. Do not organize it yet.

    Say: capture first. Every line is a chunk — a paragraph of prose, and nothing more
    is being decided yet.

    Now put the caret on one line and press Tab.

    Say: Tab makes the line above it a heading and this line its content. That is
    outlining — and here is the part worth noticing: the moment that new subchapter gets
    its first body line, it materializes as its OWN SUBDIRECTORY on disk, named with an
    order number and the title. Show the folder in the file manager beside the app.

    So the outline you just typed is a folder tree. Not a proprietary outline stored in
    a database — folders, with the names you typed.
  - |
    Type first, structure later. **Tab** turns a paragraph into a subchapter — and its
    first body line makes it a **real subdirectory**, named after its title.
- The diagram lives inside the note
  notes: |
    Open examples/66-pet-keeper-handbook and scroll to the mindmap. Then switch to the
    yamlover tab so the source of that same mindmap is visible.

    Say: when an outline wants to be a picture, the picture goes in the note as a chunk
    with its own format — here, PlantUML. Six lines of text, rendered as a mindmap. It
    diffs in git like text, because it is text.

    Say: this is also the honest slide of the deck. A native mindmap editor — dragging
    nodes around — is planned and not built. What works today is this: the diagram as a
    chunk, written by hand, rendered in place, sitting in the same file as the prose it
    belongs to.
  - |
    A chunk carries its own `(type, format)`. A `text/x-plantuml` chunk renders as a
    diagram in place — **six lines of text**, which git can diff.
- Decide
  notes: |
    Stay on this slide, then open examples/61-table.yo to show the general case.

    Say: when a decision needs more than a shrug, the shape you want is a small matrix —
    options down the side, what you care about across the top. This one is on the slide.

    Say: and the point is what the grid IS. In Markdown a table is a drawing made of
    pipe characters — the structure is fake, and nothing but the renderer understands it.
    Here the table is a yamlover node with a table schema. The rows are entries, the
    cells are entries, only the cell text is prose. Which means a cell can hold anything
    a chunk can hold — including another table — and the whole thing is still editable
    as a grid.
  - !!<*yamlover: $defs: table>
    title: Where should this note live
    header:
      - Option
        width: 3
      - Findable later
        width: 3
      - Survives the tool
        width: 3
    - - a folder of files
      - yes — the filename is the key
      - yes — nothing to export
    - - a note in an app's database
      - only through that app
      - only if it can export
    - - a chat message to yourself
      - not really
      - as long as the chat lives
- Track
  notes: |
    Open examples/73-dev-board. Let the lanes load, then drag one card to the next lane.

    Say: this folder holds five files. Each one is a task — a chapter with a few extra
    keyed fields, priority and due and assignee. The folder itself carries one line in
    its overlay saying which workflow it uses, and the workflow's states become the
    lanes, in order.

    Now drag a card. Say: that drag rewrote one annotation in one file — the task's state.
    No board database, no ticket ids, no sync. The board is a VIEW of a folder, and the
    same folder still opens as a plain file grid.
  - |
    A folder of task files **is** a board. Lanes come from the workflow's states; dragging
    a card rewrites that task's state annotation — one line, in one file.
- Tag it, then find it
  notes: |
    Open examples/67-pdf-tags and click a tag chip in the node header — the tag's own
    page opens as a grid of everything filed under it. Then click into the breadcrumb
    and type a query.

    Say: folders make you choose one place for a thing. Tags do not — a tag is a node
    like any other, and a tag chip on a document is an annotation pointing at it. Click
    the chip and you get the tag's page: a grid of everything filed there, gathered from
    all over the tree.

    Now the breadcrumb. Say: this row of cells is not just a path, it is a query editor.
    Type three dots and a type test and the tree filters to every binary under here.
    Same grammar as a path, because a path is just a query with one answer.
  - |
    A tag is a node; filing something is an **annotation** pointing at it. Click a tag
    chip for a grid of everything filed there.
  - |
    The **breadcrumb is a query editor** — `...` descends recursively, `!!<type: binary>`
    tests a type, and the tree filters to the matches.
- Six jobs, one folder
  notes: |
    Go back to the folder you started with, now full, and show it in the file manager
    next to the app.

    Say: everything from the last five minutes is in this folder. The outline is
    subdirectories. The diagram is text in a file. The decision is a table node. The
    board is five files and one line of overlay. The tags are annotations, which are
    also files.

    Which means you can zip it and mail it, commit it and see a readable diff, open it
    on a machine that has never heard of this program and still read your notes. The
    tool is optional. The notes are not.

    Next time — files and folders as the database, and what a concrete actually is.
  - |
    An outline is subdirectories. A diagram is text. A board is five files. **Zip the
    folder and mail it** — the notes stay readable without the tool.
  - Next up — files and folders as the database
